WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.268
New this morning, Donald Trump's

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.838
legal team is asking the judge

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:05.905
to hold off

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:07.507
on setting a trial date

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:08.408
in the former president's

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:09.843
classified documents case.

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:10.710
In a late night

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:11.711
court filing, Trump's

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:12.379
legal team

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:14.414
said the presidential election cycle

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:15.348
in the way they put

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.383
it is will create

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:19.419
extraordinary challenges in the jury.

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:21.154
Selection process.

00:00:21.154 --> 00:00:22.122
They want the trial delayed

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:24.924
until after the 2024 election, contending

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:25.925
that otherwise

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:27.560
it will be virtually impossible

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:28.728
to seat an impartial jury.

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:29.496
While Trump is running

00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:30.563
for the White House.

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:31.798
Lawyers for Trump Aide

00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:32.799
While Nevada

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:34.968
are also looking for a delay.

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:36.236
CNN's Paula

00:00:36.236 --> 00:00:38.071
Reid has much more on this one.

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:39.272
Paula, what are you hearing from

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:40.540
the special counsel

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.510
with regard to both of these requests?

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:43.910
Okay.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.111
Jack Smith has made it clear

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:47.180
he wants a, quote, speedy trial.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.382
He has suggested doing this in December,

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:50.984
which feels like it's a ways away.

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:53.086
But given the complexities of this case,

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:55.688
that would be indeed a speedy trial.

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:56.990
But the former president

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:58.291
has every incentive

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.492
to want to delay this until

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:00.827
after the election.

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:02.395
Now, you can't just tell the judge,

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:03.363
hey, it's not convenient

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:04.798
for me to do this right now.

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:05.999
You have to make legal argument.

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:07.434
So that's what they're putting forth

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:08.201
right now.

00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:09.769
They are arguing that this case

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:10.770
is going to raise

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.472
unprecedented questions

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:14.107
that will have to be litigated

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.341
and that will take time.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:16.876
They've also raised this question

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:18.578
about whether you can even seat

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:20.113
an impartial jury

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:22.248
leading up to something as consequential

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:24.317
as a presidential election.

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:26.286
They also laid out about half a dozen

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:27.854
other legal challenges

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:28.955
that they intend to bring.

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:29.923
In this case,

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:31.691
everything came from questions

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:32.926
about whether these documents

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:34.427
were really classified

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:35.895
to trying to challenge

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:38.031
the authority of the special counsel

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:38.965
Now, it's not likely

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:40.166
they're going to succeed

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:41.301
with those challenges,

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:43.336
but they have every right to bring them

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.271
and they have the effect

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.573
of delaying this. Right.

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:48.241
This takes time for the courts

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:50.343
to hash out these questions.

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:51.344
And that's what Jack

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:52.345
Smith was worried about.

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:52.712
That's why

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.180
one of the few things he said publicly

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.849
is that he wanted this to move quickly,

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:58.017
that that is in the public interest

00:01:58.017 --> 00:01:59.652
So the final word, though, lies

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:00.887
with the judge and everybody

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:01.988
we were watching.

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:03.590
Trump appointed Eileen Cannon

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:04.424
as she decides

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:05.191
whether she is

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.626
going to let this

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:08.495
sort of play out over a few years

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:09.796
or she, too, is going to push

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:11.064
for a speedy trial.

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:11.898
And finally, just one

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:12.499
example of

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:14.300
this is they had a hearing scheduled

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.702
for this Friday.

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:17.570
Well, not wanted to delay that.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.339
And the special counsel pushed back.

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:20.373
Absolutely not.

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.275
They really went to the mat over this.

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:22.976
They said that it is

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:23.977
in the public interest

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:25.545
not to keep delaying things

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:27.080
because he knows that you have

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.681
of course, these big questions.

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:29.949
Right, about being able

00:02:29.949 --> 00:02:31.384
to seat an impartial jury.

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:32.785
But even these little incremental

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:34.020
scheduling delays,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.288
these add up over time.

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:37.157
So as of now, it appears at that hearing

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:39.092
will be next week instead.

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:40.527
So not a long delay.

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:41.361
But again, over time,

00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:42.929
these little delays add up

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:44.697
and it's up to the judge to decide

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:46.533
when this goes to trial.

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:47.133
That's right.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:47.734
Good to see you, Paul.

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:49.769
Thanks for the update, Sara.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:50.436
With us now

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:51.037
is CNN legal

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:52.739
analyst and former federal prosecutor

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:53.873
Eliot Williams.

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:55.141
Thank you so much for joining us

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:56.643
today, Eliot.

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:57.110
First, let's

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:57.477
just get

00:02:57.477 --> 00:02:59.212
to the likelihood of all of this.

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:01.381
What is the likelihood that Judge Cannon

00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:03.583
will agree to postponing this? Really?

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.152
Trump is asking until after

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:07.520
the presidential election.

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:09.589
You know, Sara,

00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:11.457
that's a political question,

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:12.859
not a legal one now.

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:14.928
Certainly any criminal defendant

00:03:14.928 --> 00:03:17.397
has a right to a fair trial

00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:18.932
at a reasonable time

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:19.966
now trying to postpone it

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:21.201
until after the election.

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:22.569
I think poses a few problems

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:23.603
for President Trump.

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:25.104
I think, number one,

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:26.606
when it comes to juries,

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:28.541
The law presumes and anticipates

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.410
that jurors can be fair

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.945
if instructed as to what their duties

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:32.745
and obligations are.

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.347
And a lot of jurors come in and say,

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:35.548
I could never

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:36.950
hold this person in judgment.

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.184
And then the judge explains to them

00:03:38.184 --> 00:03:39.986
what the law is and says, look,

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:41.120
you have to do that.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.589
And often the jurors will agree to it.

00:03:42.589 --> 00:03:43.289
So No.

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:43.790
One, I think

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.658
they actually probably can find a fair

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:48.328
jury in a county of 1.1 million people.

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:50.163
You can find 12 folks to do it.

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:51.731
The other question is that, you know,

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:53.533
Trump is quite literally

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:55.201
the most famous person on the planet,

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:56.536
or at least one of them.

00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:57.971
Whether he's tried

00:03:57.971 --> 00:03:59.839
prior to the election or not,

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:01.174
whether he's a candidate or not,

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:03.843
his fame is still going to be an issue.

00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:05.912
So this whole idea

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:06.813
that he just can't get a fair

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:07.714
trial anywhere in Atlanta,

00:04:07.714 --> 00:04:09.115
I think is not

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.416
not going to hold much water.

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:11.517
You make a really good point.

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.152
I mean, his name is known worldwide,

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:14.420
and that's not going to change

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:15.421
during the election.

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:16.656
I want to ask you about Trump's aide.

00:04:16.656 --> 00:04:17.156
Well, not it

00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:18.691
because he is asking for delay

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:19.926
for different reasons

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:21.561
when you see these two things coming.

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:22.495
Obviously, Donald Trump

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:24.264
helping to to pay for his

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:25.832
his attorneys as well.

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:26.933
Do you think these two moves

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.902
are really just a tactic to delay or

00:04:30.203 --> 00:04:31.638
is this a reasonable request

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:34.107
with not a saying, look, my attorney,

00:04:34.107 --> 00:04:36.542
my main attorney is doing something else

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:38.544
at the time, trying something else.

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:41.347
But the prosecution says he has a Florida

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:42.882
attorney that can do just fine.

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:45.551
Sara Sidner, stop.

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:46.653
You're both right,

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:47.720
because, number

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.922
one, as a criminal defendant,

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:52.458
he's entitled to a fair trial.

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:54.127
And I think prosecutors and judges

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:55.428
are often very careful

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:56.195
on issues like this

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:57.463
because the one thing

00:04:57.463 --> 00:04:58.798
that can mess up a conviction

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:00.300
and get it thrown out on appeal

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.134
is jeopardizing

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:02.335
or violating a defendant's

00:05:02.335 --> 00:05:02.935
speedy trial

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:04.637
or just trial rights in general.

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:05.605
So I think the proceeding

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:06.706
with a little bit of caution

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:07.273
now, look,

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:08.308
it does appear that

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:08.708
he's trying

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:10.443
to slow these proceedings down

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:10.977
quite a bit

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:12.145
with a number of these pleadings

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:12.612
and filings.

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:14.047
At a certain point,

00:05:14.047 --> 00:05:15.915
the judge can just set a trial date

00:05:15.915 --> 00:05:18.017
and say and document on the record.

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:19.786
These are all the opportunities

00:05:19.786 --> 00:05:20.720
that the prosecution

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:21.821
has given the defendant.

00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:23.156
This is when all of our hearings

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:24.757
where this is, in fact a fair

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:26.292
trial date and put it on the calendar

00:05:26.292 --> 00:05:27.126
and see what happens.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.261
But but again,

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:28.961
both see

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:29.762
both the prosecutor

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:30.963
and the judge have to proceed

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:32.699
with a little bit of caution

00:05:32.699 --> 00:05:33.866
because this could really jeopardize

00:05:33.866 --> 00:05:34.567
the case in the end

